    "case_summary": "A 75-year-old Caucasian female, who does not smoke, presented for a gastroenterology evaluation due to rectal bleeding. She has mild essential hypertension, which is well controlled with diltiazem, and intermittently uses oral aspirin, 81 mg per day. In 1966, she underwent a total abdominal hysterectomy, during which both ovaries were removed. At the time of evaluation, she occasionally used supplemental estrogen but was uncertain about the start date. Her only other surgery was an uncomplicated laparoscopic cholecystectomy in 1996. The patient's BMI was 26. Vital signs and physical examination were normal, except for a heme-positive stool. An abdominal CT scan showed no significant abnormalities, but a pelvic CT scan revealed a 7 cm partially calcified mass at the upper pelvic inlet, indistinguishable from the bowel.\n\nUltrasound of this lesion confirmed a 74 × 45 × 49 mm heterogeneous (partially echogenic) mass in the upper right pelvis. The specimen demonstrated positive vimentin staining for mesenchymal spindle and round cells, consistent with benign ovarian stromal neoplasm.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
